# Keep CHANGELOG.md in sync with code changes

`CHANGELOG.md` is the public-facing record of every notable change in this
project. It is written in plain English for the open-source community — not
for internal developers — so entries must be clear, concise, and
non-technical where possible.

## Format

Each entry is grouped under a **date heading** (`## YYYY-MM-DD`) and
contains a short **section title** (`### Title`) summarising the theme of
the changes, followed by bullet points describing what was added, changed,
or fixed.

```markdown
## 2026-08-13

### Short Descriptive Title

- Added a new `Foo` component for displaying bar charts.
- Fixed a bug where clicking "Save" did nothing on the settings page.
- Updated the `corporate` theme colours for better contrast.
```

## Rules

1. **Every notable change gets a changelog entry.** If your change is
   user-facing — a new feature, a bug fix, a breaking change, a removed
   feature, or a significant refactor — add it to `CHANGELOG.md` in the
   same commit.

2. **Use today's date.** Add your entry under today's date heading. If the
   heading already exists, append to it. If not, create a new one at the
   **top** of the file (below the introductory paragraph).

3. **Write in plain English.** Describe *what changed and why it matters*,
   not *which files were edited*. Avoid jargon. The audience is an
   open-source user reading the changelog to decide whether to upgrade.

4. **Keep entries concise.** One to two sentences per bullet. Link to
   issues or PRs if helpful, but the bullet should stand on its own.

5. **Group related changes.** If a single effort touched multiple things
   (e.g. a new component plus its documentation), combine them under one
   descriptive section title rather than listing each file change.

6. **Call out breaking changes.** Start the bullet with **Breaking:** if
   the change requires users to update their code.

7. **Skip internal-only changes.** Pure refactors with no user-visible
   effect, lockfile-only updates, CI config tweaks, and test-only changes
   do not need a changelog entry unless they fix a user-reported issue.

## What counts as "notable"

| Change type | Add to changelog? |
|---|---|
| New component, prop, builtin, or theme | Yes |
| Bug fix affecting user behaviour | Yes |
| Breaking change (renamed API, removed feature, changed default) | Yes — mark as **Breaking** |
| Security fix | Yes |
| Documentation improvement (new page, major rewrite) | Yes, briefly |
| Version bump / release | Yes (note the version) |
| Dependency upgrade (user-visible, e.g. Vite major version) | Yes |
| Internal refactor with no visible effect | No |
| Test-only changes | No |
| Lockfile-only updates | No |
| CI / build config tweaks | No |

## Don't

- Don't forget to update `CHANGELOG.md` — it is the first place users and
  contributors look to understand what changed.
- Don't write entries in developer-speak. "Refactored the morph reconciler's
  attribute sync pass" means nothing to a user — say "Fixed open menus
  closing unexpectedly during re-renders" instead.
- Don't duplicate the git commit message verbatim. The changelog is a
  curated summary, not a commit log.
- Don't backfill entries for old commits. Only document changes as they
  happen.
